WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:04.416
Hello! Welcome in the next episode of Bug
 Bounty Reports Explained.

00:00:04.708 --> 00:00:08.875
Today you'll learn how Augusto
 Zanellato accidentally

00:00:09.041 --> 00:00:12.625
found a bug that allowed him
 to add a backdoor to Shopify.

00:00:13.125 --> 00:00:16.708
Make sure to stick until the end,
 because I will show you step by step

00:00:16.708 --> 00:00:18.000
how you can check 

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.125
the contents of Electron application
 no matter what platform you use.

00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:26.750
Links to the original report and Augusto's
 media are in the description.

00:00:26.875 --> 00:00:29.833
Enjoy! Electron is a Node.js

00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:32.958
framework that allows building
 desktop applications

00:00:32.958 --> 00:00:36.208
using the same technologies
 as when developing websites.

00:00:36.791 --> 00:00:42.375
Visual Studio Code, Slack, Discord
 or WhatsApp are all built using Electron.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:47.250
The minimalistic
 hello word app doesn't need much files.

00:00:47.791 --> 00:00:49.750
However, when you want to distribute, 

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.666
your application, files
 must have a specific structure.

00:00:54.208 --> 00:00:58.500
In case of this report,
 the app was packaged for Mac OS system.

00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:01.500
You can use electron-packager module.

00:01:02.041 --> 00:01:05.750
This is the command that I used
 to package my application.

00:01:06.625 --> 00:01:11.541
These options are not relevant
 for this part, at least not yet.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.125
Then you need to create a 

00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:17.666
single .DMG file using yet another module.

00:01:18.791 --> 00:01:22.208
After you mount it,
 you can finally use the application.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.541
It's inside /Volumes directory.

00:01:26.375 --> 00:01:27.625
Let's browse it. 

00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:31.708
The binary is inside
 Contents/macOS directory.

00:01:32.375 --> 00:01:37.875
For us, however, the most important
 is the Resources dir. Inside,

00:01:37.958 --> 00:01:42.375
you have the app folder that contains
 all the files from your application.

00:01:43.208 --> 00:01:45.708
However, the application
 that is the cause of

00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:48.916
this vulnerability
 did not have such directory.

00:01:49.291 --> 00:01:52.375
Instead, it had app.asar file.

00:01:53.958 --> 00:01:56.083
It's an Electron archive. 

00:01:56.083 --> 00:02:00.958
It concatenates all the files
 from your application into a single file.

00:02:01.916 --> 00:02:04.750
Somewhat like tar,
 but without compression.

00:02:05.666 --> 00:02:10.291
To modify my application
 so that it also uses asar file

00:02:10.416 --> 00:02:14.833
I had to add --asar flag
 to electron-packager command.

00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:18.000
But what's the purpose of that? 

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.208
To answer that question, I took a look
 into the Electron documentation.

00:02:23.291 --> 00:02:27.750
Until the 2nd December
 2020, the documentation said

00:02:28.416 --> 00:02:32.000
"you can also package your app
 into an asar archive

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.625
to avoid exposing your app's
 source code to users".

00:02:37.041 --> 00:02:38.708
Which was not true. 

00:02:38.708 --> 00:02:42.208
You can easily extract
 the contents of the archive

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:45.125
using "npx asar extract" command.

00:02:45.958 --> 00:02:51.250
Using this file format
 does not protect the code in any way.

00:02:52.791 --> 00:02:57.083
The documentation was changed
 only two months prior to this bug.

00:02:57.916 --> 00:03:02.000
Now it says "you can package
 you app into an asar archive

00:03:02.125 --> 00:03:06.708
to improve the performance of reading
 files on platforms like Windows".

00:03:07.958 --> 00:03:11.083
It's very interesting
 how misguiding it was.

00:03:12.208 --> 00:03:16.166
If you enjoy that I dig deep
 to find out such information

00:03:16.291 --> 00:03:19.000
make sure to like this video right now.

00:03:21.041 --> 00:03:23.416
Let's come back to our hunter. 

00:03:23.416 --> 00:03:26.666
He downloaded the application
 and was analyzing it.

00:03:27.416 --> 00:03:30.000
The name of the application
 is not disclosed

00:03:30.083 --> 00:03:33.541
but it was a random application
 from the Internet and the hunter

00:03:33.541 --> 00:03:37.416
did not know it was related
 to Shopify in any way.

00:03:38.791 --> 00:03:41.125
He just wanted to see how it works.

00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:44.666
So he started to review the contents
 of the application,

00:03:44.666 --> 00:03:46.791
including the asar archive.

00:03:48.208 --> 00:03:52.458
Inside, he found .env file. What is that?

00:03:53.083 --> 00:03:57.000
One of the most popular
npm packages is dotenv.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.583
It automatically loads
 environment variables

00:04:00.708 --> 00:04:04.058
from .env file into process.env.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.458
So when you had TEST
 the variable in the .env

00:04:07.458 --> 00:04:12.869
file, it's automatically loaded
 into the process.env.TEST.

00:04:13.458 --> 00:04:16.000
We can't be 100% sure

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.125
this application did use this package,
 but one way or another,

00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:24.833
it used environmental variables
 from the .env file.

00:04:24.833 --> 00:04:27.625
Inside, among other credentials,

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:30.583
the hunter found a GH_TOKEN variable.

00:04:32.041 --> 00:04:35.625
As you might guess,
 it was a GitHub rest API token.

00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:41.208
Augusto confirmed its valid
 by hitting /user endpoint.

00:04:41.875 --> 00:04:47.166
At this point, it was only affecting
 the developer who created the application,

00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:52.000
and nothing foreshadowed
 that it affected Shopify in any way.

00:04:53.541 --> 00:04:57.583
Then, he tried to
 query /user/orgs endpoint.

00:04:57.583 --> 00:05:00.833
And saw that this developer was a member

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.625
of Shopify organization on GitHub.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.500
So it was looking
 very promising at that point.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.583
As the next step,
 he queried /orgs/shopify/repos.

00:05:12.708 --> 00:05:17.583
And what he saw was the list of many
 public and private Shopify

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:20.625
repositories with pull and push access.

00:05:21.833 --> 00:05:24.083
The impact of this is huge.

00:05:24.375 --> 00:05:28.583
You can read all the code of Shopify
 closed-source repositories,

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:33.708
but also you can commit a backdoor
 to it, stealing millions of dollars

00:05:33.708 --> 00:05:37.708
and personal information
 of millions of Shopify customers.

00:05:38.333 --> 00:05:41.541
The Shopify team was quick
 to respond on Hackerone.

00:05:41.833 --> 00:05:45.000
The report was triaged
 as critical the same day

00:05:45.208 --> 00:05:49.250
and the $50,000 bounty
 was awarded a week later.

00:05:50.875 --> 00:05:53.291
How you can test for this bug? 

00:05:53.291 --> 00:05:58.666
After downloading .DMG file,
 you can unpack it using 7z command

00:05:58.666 --> 00:06:03.750
both on Mac OS and on Linux
 or using 7zip program on Windows.

00:06:05.125 --> 00:06:08.375
Then, you browse to the Contents/Resources

00:06:08.375 --> 00:06:12.041
and you run "npx asar
 extract app.asar app".

00:06:12.833 --> 00:06:16.541
Then you will have the sources
 of the application and you can review them

00:06:16.541 --> 00:06:19.666
as you normally would.
 What an amazing bug!

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.500
I also interviewed Agusto in my Bug
 Bounty Reports Discussed podcast.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:29.208
Among others, you can hear about why
 did he choose to review this application

00:06:29.416 --> 00:06:32.833
or what was his and his friend's
 reaction to finding

00:06:32.833 --> 00:06:35.500
the $50,000 bug by accident.

